{
  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "i144",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Changing an attribute's opening quote to { (padding={small\" ...) draws the rest of the tag's pills over the wrong words",
      "excerpt": "Changing an attribute's opening quote to { (padding={small\" ...) draws the rest of the tag's pills over the wrong words",
      "text": "The curlies should look like part of the white bubble like the quotes do.  Also, I still want an easy way to switch from quotes to curlies, make a suggestion.\n\nAlso, inside HTML, dynamic code should be inverted.\n\nDo this here, language is busy",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-15T21:12:10.053Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-15T21:42:50.310Z",
          "html": "<p>Done for the curlies and the inverted code (commit fb3a41390); a suggestion for switching below.</p>\n<p><b>The curlies, part of the bubble</b></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Every <code>{…}</code> in a drawing's markup is now ONE pill, its <code>{</code> and <code>}</code> inside it.</li>\n  <li>They're drawn in the pill's own ink, a little faded, as a quoted value's curly marks belong to its white.</li>\n  <li>The same in the code editor and on the page.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Code inside HTML, inverted</b></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The pill is dark with light words in light mode, and light with dark words in dark mode.</li>\n  <li>Its colour is a deep shade of the tag's (or block's) colour.</li>\n  <li>It's code between tags too now, <code>&lt;text&gt;{its name}&lt;/text&gt;</code>, not only an attribute's value.</li>\n  <li>Recorded as J124.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>The bug itself (I144)</b></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>padding={small\" spacing=\"xs\"&gt;</code> no longer shifts the pills: <code>xs</code> sits on its own word.</li>\n  <li>A <code>{</code> with no <code>}</code> is drawn as a mark alone, and the <code>\"</code> after <code>small</code> as plain text.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>\n  Pictures:\n  <a href=\"/epics/editor/tests/I144-editor-after-light.png\">editor, light</a>,\n  <a href=\"/epics/editor/tests/I144-editor-after-dark.png\">dark</a>;\n  <a href=\"/epics/editor/tests/I144-page-after-light.png\">page, light</a>,\n  <a href=\"/epics/editor/tests/I144-page-after-dark.png\">dark</a>.\n</p>\n<p><b>An easy way to switch from quotes to curlies: three options</b></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>\n    <b>Type it (recommended).</b> With the caret in a quoted value, typing <code>{</code> turns its two quotes into\n    braces: <code>padding=\"small\"</code> becomes <code>padding={small}</code>. Typing <code>\"</code> in a <code>{…}</code>\n    value turns it back.\n    <ul>\n      <li>Nothing to learn or find: it's the key you'd press anyway.</li>\n      <li>It ends the half-typed state that caused I144.</li>\n      <li>Undo puts back the plain <code>{</code>, for the rare time you wanted one in the text.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <b>A menu item.</b> Right-click a value, or the lightbulb: \"Make it code\" / \"Make it text\".\n    Easy to find, but two steps for a small change.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    <b>⌘-click a quote mark or brace</b> to flip the value between the two.\n    Fast, but nobody would guess it.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n<p>My pick: 1, with 2 as well, so the menu teaches the shortcut.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
